 negligence breach of  contract and breach of fiduciary duty  hopkins and torrey hinkle who is also  named as a defendant  agreed to an interview with the i-team  to respond to the allegations  hopkins says she was in charge to me the  truth is on my side what we found  two different versions of the same  events yeah  i had all sorts of confidence in her  it felt like a friendship you know  according to the lawsuit on or about  april 4 hopkins began suspecting saurs  was having twins  but failed to transfer care it continues  on may 2nd  an ultrasound which confirmed she was  carrying twins who were shearing  the same sack and placenta referred to  as monomono  according to columbia university's  obstetrics and gynecology website  for twins sharing the same sac and  placenta there's a chance their  umbilical cords could be entangled  and lead to the loss of one or both of  their lives  that's why this type of pregnancy is  closely monitored  the lawsuit alleges sauers then  anticipated having to register at a  hospital  but hopkins asserted home birth was  still an option  and had you seen an ob gyn at all  no hopkins and hinkle describe sours as  being resistant to take their advice  their attorney says there is still no  confirmation the twins shared the same  sack and placenta  because he claims sauvers refused to get  a diagnostic ultrasound  hopkins says sauers was resistant to  getting an ultrasound throughout the  pregnancy  even after her recommendations starting  at 20 weeks  if there are so many risks is it time to  say  you need to deliver at the hospital well  i guess that wouldn't be left up to me i  would never  again you're talking about removing  parents choices  by telling them what they can and can't  do hopkins says a major principle of  midwifery  is body autonomy while sowers chose  midwifery over a medical doctor  she is now questioning hopkins and her  team's qualifications  how much research did you do  about qualifications and her background